# Carrier Wound #40: The Seed Granted Mount To One Actor And The Live Run Executed As Another.

The D3 burn-in restarted after the wound #38 dream capture. Run 1 (dream) reached
`captured`. Run 2 — the **support-sweep** shape — went terminal one gate before
the planner even ran, at admission:

```
run blocked: entitlement_missing
  "Actor cannot mount requested packages: workflow/aihero-support-sweep"
```

The first reflex was "the cartridge was never seeded." **Refuted by receipt:** the
14 Jun 18:28 `pnpm app:deploy:seed` receipt shows `workflow/aihero-support-sweep`
`updated`, `entitlementCount: 1`. The row exists. The package is seeded. The
admission gate is working **correctly** — it checked `can_mount` for the actor's
subject and found no grant. The block is true. The bug is upstream of the block.

## The wound: two scripts, one identity, hardcoded twice, drifted apart

The deploy-seed path and the live-submit path each decide an actor identity, and
they decided it **independently**:

- **Seed** (`scripts/workflow-app-deploy-and-seed.mjs` → `packageSeedRequestBody`)
  granted entitlements to subject `actor:operator` (plus schema defaults). One row
  per package, `subject_id = "actor:operator"`, `can_mount = 1`.
- **Submit** (`scripts/workflow-app-run.ts` → `actorForLiveRun`) builds the actor
  a LIVE run executes as with `id: "actor:workflow-live-operator"`.

Admission resolves the executing actor's subject (`actor:workflow-live-operator`),
joins `package_entitlements`, filters `e.can_mount = 1` on
`(subject_type, subject_id)` — and finds the only seeded row is for a **different
subject**, `actor:operator`. No grant for the identity that is actually running.
Block. The two string literals were the same *intent* — "the operator that drives
seeds and live runs" — written in two files, and they **drifted**: at some point
the live-run actor was renamed/specialized to `actor:workflow-live-operator` and
the seed body was never updated to match. The seed kept returning green
(`entitlementCount: 1`, `updated`) the entire time, because the seed is not wrong
about *what it was told to grant* — it is wrong about *who actually runs*, and it
has no way to know.

This is the **polite-fakes franchise wound in identity form**: the seed receipt is
politer than production. It reports success against the subject it was handed, not
against the subject that will mount. A green receipt that does not exercise the
real executing identity is a receipt that can lie, and this one did.

> "The canonical use case for typeof is if you have a single value that you'd like
> to be the source of truth for a type (perhaps it's some kind of schema or API
> specification). By making the value the source of truth, you avoid duplication in
> defining the type."
> — Dan Vanderkam, *Effective TypeScript, 2nd Edition*, Item 15: Use Type
>   Operations and Generic Types to Avoid Repeating Yourself

Vanderkam is talking about types, but the leverage point is the same one Meadows
would name: **duplicated truth diverges.** Two hardcoded copies of "the operator
identity" is two sources of truth, which is zero sources of truth. The fix is to
make there be exactly one.

## The fix: one module both scripts import — the identities cannot disagree

`scripts/workflow-live-subjects.mjs` is the single source of truth for the
workflow subject identities, written as plain `.mjs` so **both** a plain-node
deploy script (`.mjs`) and a tsx submit script (`.ts`) can import it. A sibling
`scripts/workflow-live-subjects.d.mts` carries the declarations so the `.ts`
caller typechecks cleanly under NodeNext (allowJs off).

- `liveRunOperatorSubjectId = "actor:workflow-live-operator"` — exported once.
  `workflow-app-run.ts`'s `actorForLiveRun` now sets `id: liveRunOperatorSubjectId`
  instead of a bare literal.
- `packageSeedSubjects()` returns the per-package grant set the deploy-seed path
  provisions for **every** package. It mirrors the proven-working memory-fabric
  grant exactly:
  - `actor:operator` — mount, no invoke
  - `actor:workflow` — mount, no invoke (internal in-graph mounts)
  - `actor:workflow-live-operator` — **mount AND invoke** (the live executing
    identity)
- `workflow-app-deploy-and-seed.mjs`'s `packageSeedRequestBody` now returns
  `{ subjects: packageSeedSubjects({ operatorSubjectId: env..., operatorSubjectType: env... }) }`.

Now the seed grants `can_mount` to the exact subject the submit path runs as,
**because both read the same constant.** Renaming the live-run actor is a
one-line edit in one file that both paths pick up; they can no longer drift,
because there is nothing to keep in sync.

### Grant the subject — do NOT loosen the gate

The standing rule holds: *a verifier with no tools reviews nothing; tool/mode
constraints must be scoped per-lane, never blanket-applied.* Its admission twin:
the `can_mount` gate is **correct** and stays. The wound is fixed by **granting
the live-operator subject**, never by removing the entitlement check, widening the
subject predicate to match-any, or stamping `can_mount` true for a wildcard
subject. The gate that blocked run 2 is the same gate that will block an
*unauthorized* mount tomorrow; we did not file its teeth down, we issued the badge
the runner was missing.

## The chaos test that would have caught it (hostile transport, not a polite mock)

`tests/integration/package-seed-live-operator-entitlement.test.ts` exercises the
seed→mount handoff **end to end through both real components**, with a mutable D1
double that reproduces the production transport contract on both ends:

- The **real** seeder `finalizeCloudflarePackageSeed` → `upsertEntitlements` writes
  rows through the production insert column-order
  `(subject_type, subject_id, package_id, version_range, can_discover, can_mount, can_invoke)`.
- The **real** registry `createCloudflareD1PackageRegistryActor` → `pinPackages`
  reads them back through the production mount-check SELECT's package-id filter and
  subject-predicate bind layout.
- Nothing pre-stages rows. The double's `run()` parses the seeder's binds and the
  double's `all()` serves the registry's SELECT, replicating `queryRows`'s
  subject/package filter. The handoff is genuinely exercised; it is not a mock that
  returns a canned "pinned."
- The executing actor in the test is built with `id: liveRunOperatorSubjectId` —
  the **same shared constant** `actorForLiveRun` uses — so test and production
  cannot disagree about who runs.

Two cases, both falsifiable:

- **Grant proves out.** `seedThenMount(packageSeedSubjects())` → `status: "pinned"`,
  the live-run operator mounts. Plus a direct guard that `packageSeedSubjects()`
  contains the live operator with `canMount`. **Falsified:** drop the live-operator
  entry from `packageSeedSubjects()` and the mount blocks.
- **The wound, reproduced.** `seedThenMount([{ subjectId: "actor:operator",
  subjectType: "actor" }])` — the pre-fix operator-only body — →
  `entitlement_missing`, `"Actor cannot mount requested packages:
  workflow/memory-fabric"`. This is the exact live failure, captured as a red line.
  **Falsified by the fix:** it stays red precisely because the fix did *not* touch
  this path — it reproduces the old seed body and proves that body was insufficient.

If the seeded subject set and the executing actor identity ever drift apart again,
the grant case goes red. The drift class is now guarded, not just this instance.

`pnpm typecheck` clean · `pnpm check` clean · full suite **444 passed / 56 files**.

## The carrier discipline (nineteenth carrier wound)

#38 fixed *a correct block the operator could not read.* #40 fixes *a correct
block that should never have fired* — not by silencing it, but by issuing the
grant its target was missing. The class is not "support-sweep wasn't seeded." It
is "**the same identity was hardcoded in two scripts that provision and consume it,
so they drifted, and a green seed receipt certified a grant the live runner could
never use.**" Duplicated truth diverges; the fix collapses two copies of the
operator identity into one imported constant, so the seed and the submit cannot
disagree about who runs. The hostile double reproduces the seed→mount handoff
through both real components and pins both the grant and the wound. A seed receipt
that does not exercise the real executing identity is a receipt that can lie — and
killing that class is the whole reason the burn-in resets on a carrier failure
instead of patching past it. Linked: [[polite-fakes-franchise-wound]].

## Live outcome

**Proven on deployed code.** After `pnpm app:deploy:seed` granted
`actor:workflow-live-operator` mount on every package, the re-submitted
support-sweep run cleared the admission gate that had blocked it with
`entitlement_missing` — the wound #40 block did not recur. The fix held: the
executing identity and the seeded identity now agree because both import one
constant instead of hardcoding the subject twice.

Admission was never the only gate, though. The same run
(`run-live-20260615T021858572Z-ec8eb69c`) advanced past admission and went
terminal **one gate later**, at the executor, with
`adapter_unavailable: "Memory fabric workflow node adapter does not support
nodeType aihero.support-sweep.source-inventory."` — the aihero installer had
`return {}`-ed its adapter out of existence when its relay was unprovisioned, so
the node routed to a foreign adapter. That is a distinct class (a vanishing
adapter, not a drifted identity) and is carried as **wound #41**
([[carrier-wound-vanishing-adapter-2026-06-14]]). Wound #40 is closed: the actor
the live run executes as can now mount the packages the seed granted. The
support-sweep shape reaching `captured` waits on wound #41's fix landing and the
relay-provisioning gate it surfaces.
